The algebra in the proposed solution is essentially right up through the count. Expanding gives \(x^2+2x-1015<0\), whose roots are \(-1\pm\sqrt{1016}\approx -32.87\) and \(30.87\). The integers strictly between are \(-32,-31,\dots,30\). Checking the nearest outside values confirms the boundaries: at \(x=30\) and \(x=-32\) the product is \(945<1000\), while at \(x=31\) and \(x=-33\) it is \(1008>1000\). So the inclusive range \([-32,30]\) contains \(30-(-32)+1=63\) integers.

The solution even states 63 correctly in the body, but then contradicts itself by boxing \(\boxed{62}\) and declaring “Final answer: 62.” That final boxed answer is an off-by-one/typographical error; the correct number of integers is 63.

